WEBVTT

1
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:43.544
Get down.

2
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:48.924
I said get down.

3
00:00:49.008 --> 00:00:49.967
What?

4
00:00:51.343 --> 00:00:52.344
Get down!

5
00:01:02.521 --> 00:01:04.023
What the sh…

6
00:01:06.442 --> 00:01:07.485
That scared me!

7
00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:20.164
It's all right now.

8
00:01:21.373 --> 00:01:22.291
It's over.

9
00:01:31.383 --> 00:01:33.427
Get up slowly.

10
00:01:33.511 --> 00:01:34.595
Your muscles must be in shock.

11
00:01:44.146 --> 00:01:45.439
Thank you.

12
00:01:45.523 --> 00:01:48.692
What on earth just happened?

13
00:01:48.776 --> 00:01:50.903
The fire suppression sprinkler exploded.

14
00:01:51.737 --> 00:01:53.197
It must've been defective.

15
00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:56.826
Not even the cheapest ones
would explode like that.

16
00:01:57.827 --> 00:01:58.911
Which brand is it?

17
00:01:58.994 --> 00:02:00.788
Was it installed a long time ago?

18
00:02:01.914 --> 00:02:03.749
Did you notice anything unusual about it?

19
00:02:06.877 --> 00:02:09.213
I'm not really sure.

20
00:02:10.339 --> 00:02:11.632
When did we install it again?

21
00:02:12.299 --> 00:02:13.592
Was it about six months ago?

22
00:02:14.593 --> 00:02:16.387
We were short on cash

23
00:02:17.012 --> 00:02:19.139
-so we got a bootleg.
-What?

24
00:02:19.723 --> 00:02:21.684
"Bootleg"?

25
00:02:23.769 --> 00:02:25.521
Hey, Song-yi.

26
00:02:27.106 --> 00:02:28.649
Someone's here.

27
00:02:36.615 --> 00:02:40.202
I don't know how to express
my gratitude for your visit.

28
00:02:41.078 --> 00:02:43.581
But I must also say I'm truly sorry.

29
00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.876
There's been an unexpected issue
in the kitchen,

30
00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:49.378
and I'm afraid
we can't serve dinner tonight.

31
00:02:51.297 --> 00:02:53.924
I'm terribly sorry.

32
00:02:59.305 --> 00:03:00.931
I heard something explode.

33
00:03:01.015 --> 00:03:03.434
Is everything okay?
Should we call emergency services?

34
00:03:03.517 --> 00:03:05.269
Oh, no.

35
00:03:05.352 --> 00:03:06.854
Everything is fine now.

36
00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:08.939
The staff is cleaning up the mess.

37
00:03:09.648 --> 00:03:10.566
That's a relief.

38
00:03:12.359 --> 00:03:14.570
I don't know exactly what happened,

39
00:03:14.653 --> 00:03:16.989
but is there no way you can cook?

40
00:03:17.573 --> 00:03:20.367
We have a guest who made a long trip

41
00:03:20.451 --> 00:03:22.411
to taste Famiglia's food again
after a long time.

42
00:03:25.664 --> 00:03:26.540
Please.

43
00:03:27.249 --> 00:03:28.417
Do me a favor.

44
00:03:29.627 --> 00:03:31.045
It's been ten years.

45
00:03:38.177 --> 00:03:39.053
I'm…

46
00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.806
I'm so sorry.

47
00:03:46.352 --> 00:03:49.146
If you're an old regular
who loved our food…

48
00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:55.027
Then I really don't think
we can serve you tonight.

49
00:03:56.111 --> 00:03:57.363
Serving poor quality

50
00:03:57.947 --> 00:04:02.117
in a situation like this
would dishonor my father.

51
00:04:19.426 --> 00:04:21.136
But I understand.

52
00:04:25.057 --> 00:04:27.559
It's a shame, but I see what you mean.

53
00:04:29.853 --> 00:04:31.772
I guess we should head back then.

54
00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:34.525
Henri Bernier?

55
00:04:35.859 --> 00:04:37.152
What is he doing here?

56
00:04:38.862 --> 00:04:39.738
Who?

57
00:04:40.364 --> 00:04:41.281
Henri who?

58
00:04:46.912 --> 00:04:47.997
Are you all right?

59
00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:49.832
Did we land on some battlefield?

60
00:04:50.791 --> 00:04:52.209
Hey, are you all right?

61
00:04:53.127 --> 00:04:55.713
Get up slowly.

62
00:04:57.381 --> 00:04:58.799
Oh my god. Did you hit your head?

63
00:04:58.882 --> 00:05:00.509
-Yes.
-Let me see.

64
00:05:02.553 --> 00:05:03.554
Is it bleeding?

65
00:05:07.891 --> 00:05:08.726
Are you okay?

66
00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:11.979
Yes, I'm fine.

67
00:05:13.105 --> 00:05:14.189
I'm really fine.

68
00:05:18.152 --> 00:05:19.069
Thank you.

69
00:05:29.788 --> 00:05:32.207
I didn't expect
something like that to happen.

70
00:05:32.833 --> 00:05:35.127
Why don't we just go to Exception instead?

71
00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:36.670
We can have a glass of wine there.

72
00:05:37.463 --> 00:05:38.297
No, thank you.

73
00:05:39.548 --> 00:05:41.508
Hearing about Chef Choi's passing

74
00:05:42.509 --> 00:05:43.677
has weighed on me.

75
00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.639
I just arrived today,
so I'm tired as well.

76
00:05:49.308 --> 00:05:50.809
Let's visit Exception some other time.

77
00:05:58.150 --> 00:06:00.694
Well, if that's what you wish. No problem.

78
00:06:01.987 --> 00:06:03.781
I can catch a taxi across the street.

79
00:06:05.824 --> 00:06:08.494
Thank you for coming
to pick me up today, Ms. Baek.

80
00:06:08.577 --> 00:06:11.246
And you too, Ms. Seon.

81
00:06:12.915 --> 00:06:16.335
I'll have your luggage sent to your hotel.

82
00:06:17.336 --> 00:06:18.212
Thank you.

83
00:06:28.138 --> 00:06:31.183
Why did everything fall apart
at the last minute?

84
00:06:31.266 --> 00:06:34.103
He's our most important guest
and this is just day one!

85
00:06:34.686 --> 00:06:35.771
I swear,

86
00:06:35.854 --> 00:06:39.149
nothing ever goes right
whenever Famiglia is involved.

87
00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:43.529
You know,

88
00:06:44.071 --> 00:06:45.906
there are two types of people
in the world.

89
00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:49.493
People who make the impossible possible,

90
00:06:50.786 --> 00:06:52.913
and people who make
the possible impossible.

91
00:06:56.375 --> 00:06:58.377
Did you see that girl's facial features?

92
00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:01.296
She looks just like her father.
I bet she's just as stubborn.

93
00:07:02.631 --> 00:07:05.342
This won't be easy for me.

94
00:07:06.135 --> 00:07:08.804
My brain… I have a headache.

95
00:07:08.887 --> 00:07:11.348
Where are my antacids?
Why isn't Hyeong-koo back yet?

96
00:07:11.974 --> 00:07:14.309
What did you say? Henri Bernier?

97
00:07:14.393 --> 00:07:16.145
You mean the man who was just here

98
00:07:16.228 --> 00:07:18.647
was the world-famous chef Henri Bernier?

99
00:07:27.281 --> 00:07:28.991
Did he have to come today of all days?

100
00:07:32.035 --> 00:07:34.872
He said he missed Dad's food so much

101
00:07:35.581 --> 00:07:37.541
that he had come back after ten years.

102
00:07:38.417 --> 00:07:41.879
And I sent away
a special customer like him

103
00:07:42.921 --> 00:07:44.214
with the restaurant in this state.

104
00:07:45.299 --> 00:07:46.633
Song-yi.

105
00:07:46.717 --> 00:07:48.468
Go home and get some rest.

106
00:07:48.552 --> 00:07:50.220
The kitchen is mostly cleaned up now.

107
00:07:50.721 --> 00:07:52.556
I'll take a look at everything
thoroughly tomorrow.

108
00:07:54.474 --> 00:07:55.559
I'm sorry.

109
00:07:56.393 --> 00:07:57.644
It's all my fault.

110
00:09:13.303 --> 00:09:14.680
You're still here?

111
00:09:16.223 --> 00:09:18.558
SPICY CHICKEN WITH DRIED PERSIMMON

112
00:09:18.642 --> 00:09:21.186
I'll clean up the rest tomorrow.
You don't have to worry.

113
00:09:23.689 --> 00:09:26.024
CHICKEN SO GOOD IT STOPS A CRYING CHILD

114
00:09:26.608 --> 00:09:28.193
Sir, can you please…

115
00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.154
Why do we offer a crying child
a dried persimmon?

116
00:09:32.739 --> 00:09:36.159
Why does a child who won't stop crying
even when they hear a tiger will eat them

117
00:09:36.827 --> 00:09:38.870
only stop crying
when they're offered a dried persimmon?

118
00:09:42.374 --> 00:09:43.292
It's because…

119
00:09:47.212 --> 00:09:49.381
the best remedy for sadness…

120
00:09:53.927 --> 00:09:55.429
is nothing other than…

121
00:10:04.688 --> 00:10:06.815
delicious food.

122
00:10:12.779 --> 00:10:15.157
How long have you been cooking?
What's your position?

123
00:10:16.325 --> 00:10:17.534
I've been cooking for a year,

124
00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:19.619
and I'm still a commis.

125
00:10:19.703 --> 00:10:21.538
COMMIS:
A JUNIOR-LEVEL KITCHEN STAFF MEMBER

126
00:10:22.372 --> 00:10:26.001
By the way, what is it that you do?

127
00:10:45.562 --> 00:10:46.813
We're only serving one guest,

128
00:10:46.897 --> 00:10:49.900
but they're a super VIP,
more important than anyone.

129
00:10:51.568 --> 00:10:53.528
Today's signature course

130
00:10:53.612 --> 00:10:55.864
will have five dishes,
from an antipasto to a dessert.

131
00:10:57.741 --> 00:10:59.618
I'd like you to serve dinner tonight.

132
00:10:59.701 --> 00:11:01.912
Let's get it done in two hours, okay?

133
00:11:01.995 --> 00:11:02.996
Okay.

134
00:11:04.373 --> 00:11:05.832
First, the antipasto.

135
00:11:05.916 --> 00:11:06.750
Scallops.

136
00:11:07.501 --> 00:11:08.752
When searing scallops,

137
00:11:08.835 --> 00:11:12.047
wait until the pan is smoking hot
before you start cooking.

138
00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:14.341
-Screaming hot, okay?
-Okay.

139
00:11:15.092 --> 00:11:16.635
Sear each side for five seconds.

140
00:11:16.718 --> 00:11:19.346
SEARING:
BROWNING THE OUTSIDE OVER HIGH HEAT

141
00:11:19.429 --> 00:11:22.349
That gives you a crispy outside
and a moist, medium center.

142
00:11:27.604 --> 00:11:30.315
The scallops are only about 80% fresh, so…

143
00:11:32.609 --> 00:11:34.820
We'll go with something
other than the classic vongole.

144
00:11:35.404 --> 00:11:37.781
The clams weren't purged right,
and they smelled bad too.

145
00:11:58.885 --> 00:12:01.596
Finely slice the ingredients,
add them to a pot of oil,

146
00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:03.181
and warm them slowly over low heat.

147
00:12:03.723 --> 00:12:05.267
"Slowly over low heat."

148
00:12:07.936 --> 00:12:10.105
Then cook everything for ten minutes
over medium heat.

149
00:12:11.022 --> 00:12:11.982
And then?

150
00:12:13.358 --> 00:12:14.317
Blend it into a purée

151
00:12:14.818 --> 00:12:16.945
with a teaspoon each
of brown rice vinegar and soy milk.

152
00:12:40.010 --> 00:12:41.511
Antipasto, scallops.

153
00:12:42.554 --> 00:12:45.390
ANTIPASTO
SEARED SCALLOPS WITH CHORIZO SAUCE

154
00:13:13.084 --> 00:13:15.045
Wait, my phone.

155
00:14:02.759 --> 00:14:03.843
The scallops

156
00:14:05.262 --> 00:14:06.179
are perfect.

157
00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:11.059
They were seared to perfection,

158
00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:13.687
but they weren't as fresh
as they should've been.

159
00:14:14.813 --> 00:14:18.233
So he chose a chorizo sauce
with a strong, meaty flavor to mask it.

160
00:14:19.317 --> 00:14:21.152
Primo piatti, ravioli.

161
00:14:21.736 --> 00:14:22.612
Ravioli!

162
00:14:24.447 --> 00:14:26.741
The shrimp in the ravioli seemed spoiled.

163
00:14:27.701 --> 00:14:29.035
When were they made?

164
00:14:30.161 --> 00:14:31.580
About three days ago?

165
00:14:36.835 --> 00:14:37.669
Five days ago.

166
00:14:42.048 --> 00:14:43.049
A week ago.

167
00:14:47.137 --> 00:14:48.138
What about the pasta?

168
00:14:48.221 --> 00:14:51.975
We normally use fresh pasta, not dried,

169
00:14:52.058 --> 00:14:55.270
but our
chef de partie
quit earlier today.

170
00:15:05.905 --> 00:15:07.032
FISHCAKE

171
00:15:08.491 --> 00:15:09.534
EGGPLANT

172
00:15:14.914 --> 00:15:15.832
SUGAR

173
00:15:18.835 --> 00:15:21.129
We'll serve pasta as the primo
instead of ravioli.

174
00:15:21.212 --> 00:15:23.340
But we'll make the noodles with fishcakes.

175
00:15:24.799 --> 00:15:26.051
Fishcake noodles?

176
00:15:26.926 --> 00:15:28.970
First, let's make the tomato sauce.

177
00:16:33.159 --> 00:16:35.787
EGGPLANT PASTA WITH FISHCAKE NOODLES

178
00:16:37.789 --> 00:16:40.166
Primo, tomato pasta. Go.

179
00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:48.550
I left my phone behind.

180
00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:53.138
The primo piatti is a tomato…

181
00:16:54.180 --> 00:16:56.516
It's a tomato sauce-based pasta.

182
00:16:58.685 --> 00:17:02.939
Didn't you say your kitchen
was in no state to cook in tonight?

183
00:17:03.565 --> 00:17:06.234
It wasn't, but…

184
00:17:20.999 --> 00:17:24.419
PROJECTED REVENUE STRUCTURE
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026

185
00:17:27.464 --> 00:17:32.469
WHAT IS THE TABLE?
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026 SHOW CONCEPT

186
00:17:34.596 --> 00:17:36.890
THE WORLD'S BEST K-FOOD CHEF

187
00:17:39.058 --> 00:17:41.561
"The world's best K-food chef…"

188
00:17:58.495 --> 00:17:59.454
What's this?

189
00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:02.290
I think it's fishcake.

190
00:18:03.833 --> 00:18:04.709
Fishcake?

191
00:18:26.773 --> 00:18:28.566
The risotto rice was dry and crumbly.

192
00:18:30.235 --> 00:18:32.278
That's probably because the rice is old.

193
00:18:32.862 --> 00:18:34.989
Our supplier stopped importing
arborio rice for the risotto,

194
00:18:35.573 --> 00:18:37.659
so we had to use the cheap rice
meant for staff meals.

195
00:18:39.285 --> 00:18:41.830
Do you have any other grain,
like barley or mixed grains?

196
00:18:41.913 --> 00:18:43.665
We do have barley.

197
00:18:44.290 --> 00:18:45.875
Oh, there's millet too.

198
00:18:59.722 --> 00:19:00.557
Okay.

199
00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:29.002
The firm, springy texture
of barley and millet

200
00:19:29.878 --> 00:19:33.548
will definitely give it more chew
than a rice-based risotto.

201
00:19:35.008 --> 00:19:36.509
To maximize that texture…

202
00:19:44.017 --> 00:19:45.810
Forget the spinach. We need seafood.

203
00:19:45.894 --> 00:19:48.062
All we have are the scallops from earlier

204
00:19:48.146 --> 00:19:49.606
and the smelly clams.

205
00:19:50.440 --> 00:19:52.609
I saw a market on the way here.

206
00:19:52.692 --> 00:19:54.652
-How far is it?
-To the market?

207
00:19:55.278 --> 00:19:56.571
A quick trip takes about 20 minutes.

208
00:19:57.572 --> 00:19:58.990
-Cuttlefish.
-What?

209
00:19:59.782 --> 00:20:00.950
Cuttlefish. In ten minutes.

210
00:20:02.368 --> 00:20:04.078
Quick. Hurry. Let's go!

211
00:20:05.079 --> 00:20:05.955
Wait.

212
00:20:06.706 --> 00:20:09.459
And a bag of mixed nuts
with lots of macadamia nuts.

213
00:20:10.501 --> 00:20:11.377
And a can of peaches.

214
00:20:13.546 --> 00:20:15.256
Please. I'm counting on you.

215
00:20:18.384 --> 00:20:19.260
One more thing.

216
00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:23.556
There's a shop that sells oil.

217
00:20:24.599 --> 00:20:26.517
Ask the lady
for a bottle of fresh pine nut oil.

218
00:20:28.811 --> 00:20:29.687
Pine nut oil…

219
00:20:50.667 --> 00:20:52.752
The texture alone isn't enough.

220
00:20:53.461 --> 00:20:54.671
It needs an aroma too.

221
00:20:54.754 --> 00:20:55.922
Something powerful.

222
00:20:57.173 --> 00:20:58.132
What can I use?

223
00:21:25.868 --> 00:21:26.703
WALNUTS

224
00:22:46.657 --> 00:22:48.826
Why is he grinding that by hand?

225
00:22:49.535 --> 00:22:52.872
Does food taste better when made by hand?

226
00:22:54.457 --> 00:22:55.458
Chef?

227
00:23:03.466 --> 00:23:04.300
Bingo.

228
00:23:29.742 --> 00:23:31.536
CUTTLEFISH RISOTTO

229
00:23:43.673 --> 00:23:44.715
What is this?

230
00:23:46.509 --> 00:23:47.885
The rice and cuttlefish both

231
00:23:48.803 --> 00:23:49.762
taste insane.

232
00:23:53.641 --> 00:23:56.185
I think it's made with barley
and something else.

233
00:23:58.729 --> 00:24:00.648
What is this unique aroma, Ms. Choi?

234
00:24:36.726 --> 00:24:37.727
Latex gloves.

235
00:25:18.351 --> 00:25:20.269
Burdock purée, squeeze bottle.

236
00:25:28.819 --> 00:25:30.738
Crown daisy pesto, small bottle.

237
00:26:22.123 --> 00:26:25.584
It looks like it's done.
Why isn't he telling me to serve it?

238
00:27:12.506 --> 00:27:17.928
BLACK GOAT STEAK IN PINE SMOKE

239
00:27:27.313 --> 00:27:28.356
This is just…

240
00:27:30.358 --> 00:27:31.317
I'm lost for words.

241
00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:35.571
Every dish was based on
classic Italian recipes,

242
00:27:35.654 --> 00:27:38.741
but each was given
a distinctly modern twist.

243
00:27:39.658 --> 00:27:42.286
Famiglia is without its head chef,
Choi Seung-hyo,

244
00:27:42.370 --> 00:27:43.788
and the food is still this amazing?

245
00:27:46.749 --> 00:27:48.584
Do excuse me. I need to use the restroom.

246
00:27:48.667 --> 00:27:49.585
Oh, sure.

247
00:28:25.413 --> 00:28:26.622
CHEF CHA

248
00:28:29.917 --> 00:28:30.751
Hey, Woo-jin.

249
00:28:32.795 --> 00:28:33.629
Well…

250
00:28:36.173 --> 00:28:37.383
It's a long story.

251
00:28:38.259 --> 00:28:39.927
Can I explain to you later?

252
00:29:00.281 --> 00:29:03.075
ORDER FOR TABLE 3

253
00:29:08.497 --> 00:29:10.708
Table three, two yuzu crème brûlées
and one fried green plum ice cream.

254
00:29:10.791 --> 00:29:12.626
-How long will it take?
-Two minutes, Chef.

255
00:29:12.710 --> 00:29:15.629
Table eight is still waiting for the main.
Where are the two quails?

256
00:29:15.713 --> 00:29:18.174
They're almost done.
We'll garnish and send them out.

257
00:29:18.257 --> 00:29:19.300
Work faster, everyone!

258
00:29:20.342 --> 00:29:21.510
We're behind schedule today.

259
00:29:21.594 --> 00:29:23.053
We've got two complaints already.

260
00:29:23.137 --> 00:29:24.388
-Yes, Chef!
-Yes, Chef!

261
00:29:37.151 --> 00:29:39.195
The dishes aren't entirely unfamiliar.

262
00:29:39.987 --> 00:29:43.032
But they all tasted completely new to me.

263
00:30:02.009 --> 00:30:03.052
Excuse me.

264
00:30:03.802 --> 00:30:06.889
Are you the chef
who prepared the food tonight?

265
00:30:08.807 --> 00:30:09.642
Yes.

266
00:30:10.518 --> 00:30:13.270
I thought Famiglia
was missing a head chef.

267
00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:15.981
Are you the new chef?

268
00:30:17.942 --> 00:30:19.109
No.

269
00:30:19.193 --> 00:30:21.529
I happened to cook just for tonight.

270
00:30:22.196 --> 00:30:23.113
Then may I ask

271
00:30:23.739 --> 00:30:27.076
which restaurant you work at?

272
00:30:30.704 --> 00:30:34.250
I'm just someone who happened to stop by.

273
00:30:37.461 --> 00:30:38.546
Goodbye.

274
00:30:39.505 --> 00:30:40.422
The food tonight…

275
00:30:45.469 --> 00:30:46.637
was quite impressive.

276
00:30:47.596 --> 00:30:50.140
I had to find out who had prepared it.

277
00:30:50.224 --> 00:30:51.642
I'm sorry if I was prying too much.

278
00:31:07.241 --> 00:31:08.200
Wait.

279
00:31:08.284 --> 00:31:09.702
They just served dessert.

280
00:31:09.785 --> 00:31:11.120
I'm sorry, Ms. Choi.

281
00:31:11.203 --> 00:31:12.705
I have to rush back.

282
00:31:13.914 --> 00:31:14.999
I see.

283
00:31:15.082 --> 00:31:17.334
Thank you so much for the meal.

284
00:31:17.418 --> 00:31:18.836
I really enjoyed it.

285
00:31:19.336 --> 00:31:21.422
I'll try the dessert next time.

286
00:31:24.341 --> 00:31:25.467
Just a moment.

287
00:31:31.599 --> 00:31:32.725
You didn't have to.

288
00:31:36.854 --> 00:31:38.939
The chef put a lot of work into it.

289
00:31:39.023 --> 00:31:40.608
It would be a shame to waste it.

290
00:31:40.691 --> 00:31:43.694
Be sure to put it in the fridge
if you want to eat it tomorrow.

291
00:31:45.571 --> 00:31:46.697
Thank you.

292
00:31:48.073 --> 00:31:49.992
-I'll see you around, Ms. Choi.
-Okay.

